WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.980
Hey Thoughty2 Here.

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.779
Do you ever talk to yourself?

00:00:03.779 --> 00:00:06.919
No, not out loud, in your mind.

00:00:06.919 --> 00:00:11.179
I’m talking about that little voice in your
head that often chips in throughout the day

00:00:11.179 --> 00:00:13.080
to offer up some useful advice.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:18.390
You know, the one that’s saying “I hate
you” over and over again whilst your boss

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.940
is giving you a mighty fine rollicking.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:25.370
Sometimes we even have entire conversations
with ourselves in our heads.

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:31.310
We can also hear it when we read, it’s as
if our mind is saying the words out loud but

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:33.400
only you can hear it.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:39.520
What causes that little voice inside your
head and what’s its purpose?

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.200
Let’s find out.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:48.610
You might call it Barbara or Keith but scientists
actually have a proper name for the voice

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:49.610
in your head.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:56.320
It’s called “inner speech” and it’s
been a topic of extensive research for centuries.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:01.230
Psychologists in particular have been obsessed
with it ever since psychology began.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:07.120
In the 1930s Russian psychologist Lev Vygotsky
put forward the case that inner speech is

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:10.250
a result of the internalisation of external
speech.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:15.920
Or to put it in other words, he thought that
inner speech is the exact same process as

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:21.190
external speech, right up until the point
where noise exits our mouth.

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:23.420
There may be some truth to this theory.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:28.550
In the nineties neuroscientists used neuroimaging
techniques to discover that an area of the

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:33.600
brain called “Broca’s area” which is
responsible for fine motor skills involved

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.940
with speech, was active during inner speech
as well as normal speech.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:44.190
This means that the muscles and organs that
move when we speak, such as the larynx, also

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:50.390
move when we speak to ourselves in our minds,
despite there being absolutely no reason for

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:53.240
such muscles and organs to do so.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:57.920
This can only mean that the systems in our
brain that govern external speech and inner

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:03.200
speech are very closely linked or maybe even
the same.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.890
The two systems are so intrinsically linked
for good reason.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:12.060
Because inner speech is actually a prediction
of what we’re about to say.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.700
Researcher Mark Scott at the Department of
Linguistics at the University of British Columbia

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:21.530
led a stud which explored a brain signal known
as “corollary discharge”.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:26.810
This signal is a copy of a motor signal that
is sent to the brain to alert the brain that

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:32.860
we are about to move, so the brain knows to
react accordingly and most importantly not

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:34.570
to overreact.

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:39.290
The purpose of corollary discharge is to separate
sensory experiences that come from outside

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:42.890
our body and ones that we produce ourselves.

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:48.500
It allows our brain to predict our own movements
before we actually make them.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.810
This is why we can’t tickle ourselves.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:57.050
Because, before you even start to tickle yourself
a corollary discharge signal has already been

00:02:57.050 --> 00:03:01.900
sent to your brain to alert it that you’re
about to tickle yourself and so your brain

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:05.390
completely ignores the sensation of being
tickled.

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:10.550
The system is primarily used to determine
the difference between an external threat

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:16.730
and something that we do ourselves and should
therefore be considered non-threatening.

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:22.520
Our brain utilises corollary discharge in
a very ingenious way when we speak.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:27.970
If corollary discharge didn’t exist, when
we talk our voice would be overwhelmingly

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:28.970
loud.

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:33.660
Because our ears are so close to the source
of the sound, our own voice would appear so

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:38.129
loud that it would drown out every other noise
around us, and we wouldn’t be able to hear

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:43.710
anything but our own voice and no matter how
much some people love the sound of their own

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:46.920
voice, that would get bloody annoying.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:52.110
However, when we speak corollary discharge
is used to predict that we are going to produce

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:56.190
a noise and so our brain dampens the sound
of that noise.

00:03:56.190 --> 00:04:01.150
The result is that our own voice sounds quieter
to us than to everyone else.

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:06.830
Amazingly our brain does this self-voice filtering
without affecting the volume of voices from

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:08.349
other people.

00:04:08.349 --> 00:04:10.750
So why do we have a voice inside our heads?

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:16.640
Ours brains are able to plan sentences in
our minds before we actually say them.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.389
The brain is also really good at replaying
sounds we’ve heard before.

00:04:21.389 --> 00:04:26.089
Try imagining any sound you’ve heard before
throughout your life, be it a police siren

00:04:26.089 --> 00:04:27.919
or birds tweeting.

00:04:27.919 --> 00:04:33.069
When you think about these past sounds you
can actually hear it, it’s almost as if

00:04:33.069 --> 00:04:35.599
it’s being replayed inside your head.

00:04:35.599 --> 00:04:41.849
So when our brain plans a sentence it replays
the sentence inside our head, and this is

00:04:41.849 --> 00:04:47.379
what we refer to as the voice in our head,
or inner speech.

00:04:47.379 --> 00:04:52.749
Because of corollary discharge the brain knows
which sentences should be spoken out loud

00:04:52.749 --> 00:04:58.259
and which are meant for our ears only and
therefore should be replayed as inner speech

00:04:58.259 --> 00:04:59.690
in our mind.

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:04.729
The brain heavily utilises inner speech when
we read because it helps our brain to better

00:05:04.729 --> 00:05:08.369
understand the words on the page and remember
them.

00:05:08.369 --> 00:05:13.669
Scientists believe people with dyslexia have
a harder time reading and understanding words

00:05:13.669 --> 00:05:16.229
because they have no inner voice.

00:05:16.229 --> 00:05:20.780
It may be hard to imagine but most dyslexic
people don’t understand the concept of a

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:27.180
voice inside their head, because they quite
simply don’t have one, they instead visualise

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:31.499
and understand everything in pictures instead
of words.

00:05:31.499 --> 00:05:36.029
For most people inner voice is an automatic
process and we can hear it every minute of

00:05:36.029 --> 00:05:42.069
the day, narrating our lives, planning and
predicting what we’re about to say and communicating

00:05:42.069 --> 00:05:45.849
to us what we are reading in real-time.

00:05:45.849 --> 00:05:50.979
Some dyslexic individuals have no grasp of
this concept and simply don’t experience

00:05:50.979 --> 00:05:55.800
it, that’s why they often move their mouths
whilst reading, because they don’t have

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:01.069
the luxury of a voice inside their head reading
it for them, to help them to easier understand

00:06:01.069 --> 00:06:02.309
the text.

00:06:02.309 --> 00:06:07.080
Young children do the exact same thing because
we don’t develop the skill of an inner voice

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:09.840
until later on in childhood.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:14.460
Until children learn this skill they tend
to say everything out loud, for better or

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:15.580
for worse.

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:21.520
It may at times seem like a little devil inside
your head, coercing you to do unruly things,

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:25.530
perhaps telling you to “punch your boss
in the face, go on do it”.

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:31.339
But it is in fact just your brain planning
and even predicting what you’re about to

00:06:31.339 --> 00:06:35.379
say, even if you don’t end up actually saying
it.

00:06:35.379 --> 00:06:40.449
Because it may be the case that only you need
to hear it, and let’s be honest that’s

00:06:40.449 --> 00:06:50.219
usually for the best.

